  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nOverall trend through the week will be for below normal temperatures \nand primarily dry conditions within low level east-northeast flow on \nthe southern fringes of an expansive northern Great Lakes high \npressure system. Still can't rule out sporadic pockets of light \nshowers or sprinkles today given lingering column moisture and \nanother weak mid level disturbance drifting east atop the primary \nfrontal zone now well south of the area. Best chances, 20-40%, for \nmeasurable light rain will generally be south of the US 24 corridor \ntoday. A more pronounced shortwave just entering the northern Plains \ndoes shift east over the area Tuesday into Tuesday night. Continued \nlow level drying will keep any shower/sprinkle activity light and \nisolated/scattered at best during this time, with the bulk of the \nperiod dry and cool.\n\nA more notable injection of dry air occurs mid week through the \nweekend as an upper level blocking pattern settles in with the local \narea generally located in a subsident regime on the backside of a \ndeveloping Mid Atlantic negative height anomaly. More in the way of \nsunshine and gradual air mass modification should push highs back \ninto the low to mid 70s by this weekend.",
